\chapter{Learning to Read a Kernel}
\label{ch:reading}
\index{Lions, John}\index{Sixth Edition Unix}\index{xv6}

\section{Why a small implementation is a serious subject}

John Lions's commentary approaches Unix as something a student can read.
The subject is Sixth Edition on the PDP-11, not an abstract operating system
assembled from unrelated algorithms. A table entry leads to a scheduler;
a scheduler leads to a context switch; a context switch leads to machine
registers. The explanation gains power from following an actual working
system across those boundaries.

Its small size makes this possible, but smallness should not be confused
with triviality. A kernel mediates between independent computations and
physical devices. It must preserve protection, retain enough state to
resume interrupted work, and coordinate activity that happens at
inconvenient moments. Even a compact implementation contains subtle
interactions whose correctness depends on assumptions outside the current
function \parencite[ch. 1, 4]{lions}.

The commentary also has a history as a teaching artifact. Source licensing
restricted its circulation, and authorized teaching could be difficult to
separate from prohibited redistribution. Its later wider publication
made visible an educational demand that the earlier licensing structure
had frustrated. The famous reputation of the book is therefore partly
technical and partly a story about who could study a system.

This chapter uses Lions's method rather than reproducing his annotated
source. The reader should learn to ask what must remain true across a
transition. Once that habit is established, an unfamiliar kernel becomes
less a mass of names and more a set of responsibilities.

\section{Begin with the machine contract}
\index{PDP-11!memory management}\index{traps}

The processor defines which instructions are privileged, how interrupts
arrive, how memory addresses are translated, and what state is saved when
execution changes mode. The operating system cannot wish these rules away.
An implementation explanation that omits them can make correct code look
mysterious or incorrect code look plausible.

On the PDP-11 systems discussed by Lions, memory management uses segments
and architecture-specific registers. The familiar modern picture of a
large demand-paged virtual address space is the wrong starting point.
The kernel also works within tight address-space limits, with arrangements
for kernel state and per-process data that exploit the machine's mapping
facilities. These details explain code that would otherwise seem
needlessly indirect.

A trap is a controlled entry into privileged execution, often used to
request a system service. An interrupt usually reports an asynchronous
event from a device or timer. Both need entry and return machinery, but
they differ in origin and in what the interrupted computation expects.
The hardware saves some state; assembly and C code preserve and interpret
the rest.

The right reading order is consequently not always the order of files in
a directory. Learn the processor's contract, identify the kernel's
principal tables, trace one complete operation, then examine its exceptional
paths. This is closer to following a transaction through an organization
than to reading a novel from the first line to the last.

\section{Tables are the system's memory of itself}

A running kernel contains representations of processes, open files,
in-memory inodes, buffers, devices, and pending events. These structures
are not incidental bookkeeping. They are how the system remembers the
relationships that its interfaces promise.

In Sixth Edition, the process table and the per-process user structure
serve different roles. Information needed to manage processes must remain
accessible even when a process's larger image is swapped out. Other state
can travel with the process. That division makes sense only when read
against the memory and swapping model \parencite[ch. 7--8]{lions}.

Similarly, a descriptor number, an open-file table entry, and an inode
represent different things. The descriptor is a process-local reference.
The open-file entry holds state associated with an opening, including an
offset. The inode represents the underlying filesystem object. Combining
them in one's mental model makes inherited descriptors and unlinking
almost impossible to explain correctly.

For each structure, ask four questions: who allocates it, who may refer to
it, what protects changes to it, and when it can be reclaimed. These
questions expose lifetimes. A reference count is useful only when the
code that acquires and releases references obeys a consistent rule.
A lock is useful only when all relevant readers and writers agree on
what it protects.

\section{Trace a request, then interrupt it}

Suppose a program requests bytes from a file. The request passes through
system-call entry, argument handling, descriptor lookup, file and inode
state, block mapping, and the buffer cache. If the needed block is absent,
a device operation may be started and the process may sleep. A device
interrupt later records completion and makes waiting work runnable.
Finally the request returns bytes or an error to user space.

This trace is more informative than studying each function independently.
It reveals where the operation can block, where an object must remain
alive, and where another process can observe state. It also reveals that
a system call need not run in one uninterrupted stretch. The logical
operation can span several periods of CPU execution.

Now ask what happens at each boundary. What if a signal arrives? What if
memory allocation fails? What if another process closes its reference?
What if the device reports an error? Historical kernels and modern kernels
answer these questions differently, but the questions are stable.

Lions's treatment rewards this style of inquiry. The celebrated difficult
context-switch passage is not a license to skip understanding. It is a
warning that saved execution state, address mappings, and control flow
must be considered together. A local reading of a few C statements can
miss the operation's real meaning.

\section{Xv6 is a comparison, not a time machine}
\index{RISC-V}\index{Sv39}

The supplied xv6 text is dated 27 October 2019 and describes a RISC-V
implementation. Earlier xv6 material often used x86, and newer revisions
can change details and section numbering. This book uses the supplied
edition consistently. Its chapters on page tables, traps, locking,
scheduling, and the file system provide a second complete specimen
\parencite{xv6}.

Xv6 preserves the teaching value of a small Unix-like interface while
making different hardware assumptions. Its Sv39 page tables, supervisor
mode, trap machinery, and multicore coordination do not belong to Sixth
Edition. Its file-system log is also a teaching design, not a feature
that should be retroactively attributed to the original PDP-11 system.

The differences are the point. Both systems support processes and files;
the mechanisms maintaining those abstractions have changed.
A uniprocessor implementation can rely on facts that fail as soon as two
cores execute kernel code simultaneously. An implementation based on
segments can organize memory in ways that a page-table implementation
would not choose.

Xv6 is also intentionally incomplete as a production system. It omits
many policies and facilities required for hostile users, modern devices,
large-scale performance, and long-running service. An educational mechanism
can expose a principle without being a recommended implementation for
all workloads. The supplied book itself repeatedly distinguishes its
model from real-world systems.

\section{History is evidence, not a benchmark competition}

Comparing a modern Linux kernel with Sixth Edition by line count alone
explains little. Modern kernels support many processors, devices,
filesystems, networking protocols, security mechanisms, and compatibility
obligations that the older system did not attempt. Some complexity is
avoidable, but some records new responsibilities.

Spinellis's Unix history repository offers another way to study change:
follow artifacts and revisions across time. Its reconstruction combines
snapshots and repositories, so early commit metadata is not equivalent
to a contemporaneous developer log. The paper explicitly discusses its
construction and limitations. Historical data must be interpreted as
carefully as historical prose \parencite{spinellis}.

The practical method is to hold an abstraction steady while comparing the
work needed to implement it. How does each system prevent one process
from overwriting another? How does each remember a blocked reader?
How does each recover a file operation after a crash? Those questions
produce a useful account of progress without pretending the later system
was simply the earlier one with unnecessary decoration.

The following chapters apply that method. They move from a program's
execution, through competition for the processor, to persistent storage
and communication. At every stage the visible interface remains smaller
than the machinery required to keep its promise.
